VLHE - Virtual Legacy Hardware Emulation
========================================

Version 1.0

VLHE gives a Linux 2.2 machine the sound and CD hardware that period
programs expect, in software:

  Many programs, one sound card
      The vsound module and its pump, vsoundd, give every program its
      own /dev/dsp and mix them onto the real card. A game, a CD
      player and a MIDI synth can all play at once on a card whose
      driver allows one user.

  A CD-ROM drive made from a disc image
      The vdisc module and vdiscd present .cue, .ccd (CloneCD) and
      .iso images as /dev/vdisc0 upwards - data, and CD audio that
      plays through vsound. The control centre's CD+G viewer shows a
      disc's CD-TEXT and the graphics CD+G karaoke discs carry.
      Programs that look for /dev/cdrom can be pointed at it.

  A MIDI synthesizer
      The vmidi module and vmidid play MIDI through SoundFont 2 banks,
      so Doom, Quake and any OSS sequencer program get General MIDI on
      a card that has no wavetable.

  A control centre
      vlhe.gtk, a GTK 1.2 program in the style of the desktop's own
      control panels: load and unload, a volume mixer for every
      channel, the sound, MIDI and CD settings, a CD+G viewer that
      plays karaoke discs with their graphics, and Render MIDI, which
      turns a .mid file into a WAV (or an MP3, with LAME) without
      opening a sound device.

It was written for Corel Linux OS 1.2 (kernel 2.2.16, GCC 2.95.2, KDE
1.1) and should build on any Linux 2.2 system of the period.

Nothing of any other project's code is in VLHE. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS
lists what was studied.


Getting started
---------------

INSTALL says how to build and install it, or how to run it straight
from an unpacked folder without installing anything.

Once it is installed, run the control centre:

    vlhe.gtk

Loading modules needs root. Run vlhe.gtk as root - KDE's menu has
Applications > System > VLHE Control Centre (root), which asks for
the root password (INSTALL has why su will not do). A portable folder
can also be given a setuid test build, which asks for the root
password when it is needed (INSTALL, "The control centre as an
ordinary user"). The Status page's Load
Checked button brings up what is ticked under "Include in load";
Simulate shows every command it would run, running nothing.

From a console:

    vlhe apply          load and start everything, as root
    vlhe apply -n       show what it would do
    vlhe apply -u       stop and unload it all
    vlhe status         what is loaded and running, and what needs
                        attention
    vlhe repair         put right what needs attention, asking first
    setup-vlhe          set the machine's settings, menu-driven

Point programs at:

    /dev/dsp            sound - vsound's mixer, once loaded
    /dev/vdisc0         the first virtual CD-ROM drive
    /dev/vmidi          the MIDI synth (MIDI device 0 for most programs)

A soundfont is needed for MIDI. Set one on the control centre's Midi
Settings page; any General MIDI .sf2 file works.


If something goes wrong
-----------------------

Run sysinfo-vlhe and send us the file it writes. It reports this
machine, this copy of VLHE and what VLHE can see - the kernel, the
sound modules, the device nodes, the daemons, the drives, the settings
files and the end of the daemon log - into sysinfo-vlhe-<date>.txt in
the current directory, and shows it on the screen too. It changes
nothing on the machine; besides its report it writes only a scratch
file under /tmp, which its report mentions. Read the file before
sending it: it can contain your user
name and the names of your files.

    portable:   ./tools/sysinfo-vlhe
    installed:  /usr/local/share/vlhe/tools/sysinfo-vlhe
                (/usr/share/vlhe/tools/sysinfo-vlhe from the package)

As root it can also see what only root can read.


Requirements
------------

  - Linux 2.2 with loadable modules and OSS sound (the stock kernel
    of Corel Linux 1.2 qualifies), and a sound card with a working
    OSS driver
  - to build the modules: that kernel's source tree, configured
  - to build the control centre: GTK 1.2 and GLib 1.2 development
    files (Corel ships none - a prebuilt control centre is provided)
    Debian Potato Archive hosts 1.2.7
  - for MP3 renders: LAME, which is not included


